      "id": 11,
      "options": {
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "## Metric availability on the two runtimes\n\n- **No `jvm_info` on the JDK 8 side.** Boot 2.7 / Micrometer 1.9 exports no `jvm_info`; Boot 3.5 / Micrometer 1.15 does. This dashboard therefore identifies runtimes by the per-series `jvm_version` tag (`1.8.0_503` vs `21.0.12.1`), never by `jvm_info`.\n- **Shared families used here:** `http_server_requests_seconds_{count,bucket}`, `jvm_gc_pause_seconds_{sum,count}`, `jvm_memory_used_bytes`, `jvm_threads_live_threads`, `process_cpu_usage`. All were verified in both scrape texts.\n- **JDK 21 only, deliberately unused:** `jvm_gc_overhead`, `jvm_memory_usage_after_gc`, `jvm_info`, `process_cpu_time_ns_total` (Micrometer 1.15). Panels never fake them for the JDK 8 side.\n- **Startup time is measured by the built-in `application_ready_time_seconds`** (Actuator `StartupTimeMetricsListener`, application start to `ApplicationReadyEvent`). It exists on both Boot 2.7/Micrometer 1.9 and Boot 3.5/Micrometer 1.15, so no custom `app_startup_seconds` gauge was added; the requirement to add one only applies when no metric exists.\n- **Semantics differ on the read path:** JDK 21 serves `/api/products/{id}` through Caffeine L1 + Redis L2, the JDK 8 shell reads MySQL directly. Throughput/latency differences therefore include the cache, not only the runtime.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "title": "Reading notes (metric gaps and semantics)",
      "type": "text"
    }
